WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:08.380
Decentralized Institutes of Health Your military-industrial complex solved coordination for killing: weapons manufacturers,

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.840
generals, politicians, and factory workers all profit from the same budget, so they all show up.

3
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.980
Disease has no such coalition. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health builds one.

4
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:28.620
It does three things: receive treaty funds, allocate them via patient choice and wishocratic voting, and pay based on results

5
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:36.420
instead of grant applications. It's the Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, or SHAEF, for the war on disease.

6
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:42.380
Same selfishness as the military-industrial complex, but pointed at biology instead of each other.

7
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.140
Nobody gets paid for writing essays about why they should be allowed to try curing things.

8
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:56.020
They get paid for curing things. I have been watching your species fight disease for eighty years, and I want to describe

9
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.640
what I see, because I think if you saw it from the outside you would be embarrassed.

10
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.280
You have eight billion of people humans. Some of them are sick.

11
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:13.150
Some of them are scientists who could help the sick ones. And some of them are sitting in offices writing forty-seven-page

12
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:21.040
documents explaining why they should be allowed to try. These documents are called "grant applications."

13
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:29.240
They take six months to write and forty minutes to reject. Your scientists spend fifty to sixty-seven percent of their time

14
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:36.320
writing them. The other thirty-three to fifty percent is spent doing science, which is the thing they were hired for, though

15
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.380
at this point it's more of a hobby they squeeze in between grant applications.

16
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:48.140
I asked one of your scientists what a grant application is and she said "it's a document where you explain what you'd discover

17
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:53.300
if someone let you." Which means your scientists already know what they'd find.

18
00:01:53.440 --> 00:02:00.760
So why do they need the money? I thought about this for a long time and then I realized I'd misunderstood the question,

19
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.660
which is also what happens to most grant applications. Your N.I.H.

20
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:12.640
spends forty-seven billion dollars a year. Billions flow to projects that never produce treatments.

21
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:21.460
Your system isn't designed to cure disease. It's designed to produce grant applications, which occasionally, as a side effect,

22
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:27.420
produce medicine. This is like designing a restaurant whose primary output is menus.

23
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.580
Sometimes, by accident, food comes out. Everyone acts surprised when it does.

24
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:39.040
This is not a conspiracy. It's just what happens when you pay people for asking instead of finding.

25
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:47.100
You've built a machine whose primary output is requests for money and whose secondary output is, reluctantly, science.

26
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:53.140
On Wishonia, we have a word for systems that produce the opposite of their stated purpose.

27
00:02:53.420 --> 00:03:01.580
The word is "human." We also use it as a verb. As in, "the project was going well until someone humaned it.".

28
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:07.220
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health is what you'd build if you started over and actually meant it.

29
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.810
Here's the blueprint. The Health-Industrial Complex: Coordinating Your War on Disease.

30
00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:23.780
The Olsonian Problem. Your economist Mancur Olson identified why public goods are systematically underproduced: diffuse

31
00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:32.320
benefits and concentrated costs. In simpler terms: everyone benefits from a cure for cancer, but nobody benefits enough

32
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.640
to fight for it the way a weapons manufacturer fights for a bomber contract.

33
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:45.020
It's one of those observations that's obvious once someone says it, and invisible before, which describes most of your species'

34
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.540
problems. Curing cancer benefits eight billion people a little.

35
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:57.240
But nobody benefits enough to organize, lobby, and fight for it the way a defense contractor fights for a bomber contract.

36
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:05.240
The eight billion don't show up. This is why your species has spent fifty years "fighting cancer" while your defense industry

37
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:11.020
got stealth bombers, aircraft carriers, and G.P.S. The defense industry showed up.

38
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.980
Cancer patients were busy having cancer, which is time-consuming in a way that's hard to explain to people who haven't tried

39
00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:25.360
it, and which I understand is not recommended. Your military-industrial complex solved this problem for killing.

40
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:33.580
Weapons manufacturers, generals, politicians, and factory workers all have concentrated interests in military spending.

41
00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:42.780
They coordinate. They lobby. They win budgets. Result: the most sophisticated death-delivery infrastructure in human history.

42
00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:50.740
Also result: the most sophisticated death-delivery infrastructure in human history pointed at everyone, including the people

43
00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:58.160
who paid for it. I looked up "death-delivery" and it's not a real phrase, but it should be, because your species has been

44
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:05.380
doing it professionally since before you had writing, which means you were killing each other before you could spell "killing,"

45
00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.840
which takes some commitment. Disease has no such coalition.

46
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.940
Your patients are too sick to lobby. Your researchers compete for scraps.

47
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:22.680
Your funders lack coordination. Your politicians get no credit for cures that arrive after their term ends (which is all

48
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:29.880
of them, because cures take fifteen years and terms last four, which is a scheduling problem your species has never solved

49
00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:37.800
and never tried to). Everyone wants disease eradicated; no one has a concentrated interest in making it happen.

50
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:44.960
It's like everyone wanting a clean kitchen but nobody wanting to do the dishes, except the dishes are cancer and the kitchen

51
00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:54.020
is on fire. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will solve the Olsonian problem by creating concentrated interests in

52
00:05:54.020 --> 00:06:03.400
disease eradication. It will build a health-industrial complex that coordinates actors around eradication the way your military-industrial

53
00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.640
complex coordinates actors around making humans stop being alive.

54
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:13.620
Same structure, opposite purpose. Same selfishness, better direction.

55
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:21.280
Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, or S.H.A.E.F., for Your War on Disease.

56
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:25.360
In nineteen forty-four, Eisenhower didn't replace the Allied armies.

57
00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:32.180
He coordinated them. Set the objective, allocated resources, made sure everyone pulled in the same direction.

58
00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.420
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will be S.H.A.E.F. for the war on disease.

59
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:43.360
Pharma companies stay pharma companies. Universities stay universities.

60
00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:51.520
Patient groups stay patient groups. But they operate as one force because the coordination layer points them at the same

61
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:58.440
thing, which is the thing they were all supposedly pointed at already, except they weren't, because nobody was coordinating.

62
00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:04.060
Your war on disease has been losing for fifty years because it's not actually a war.

63
00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:11.700
It's a collection of uncoordinated skirmishes where researchers compete for grants, pharma companies hide failures, and

64
00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:21.340
patients can't access trials. You've been "fighting" cancer the way a cat "fights" a laser pointer: lots of energy, no coordination,

65
00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:29.210
nothing caught. Imagine if D-Day had been run this way. The Americans land at one beach, the British at another.

66
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:35.500
Neither tells the other what they learned. Both compete for the same supply ships while the enemy reads their grant applications.

67
00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:44.160
This is how your species currently runs medical research, and you've been doing it long enough that it feels normal, which

68
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.260
is the most dangerous thing about it. The R.O.I. Maximization Protocol.

69
00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:54.860
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will not be a platform.

70
00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:58.460
It won't be an organization. It won't even really be a thing.

71
00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:04.780
It will be a coordination protocol, which means it's the rules by which the things talk to each other.

72
00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.880
On Wishonia, we built ours four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years ago.

73
00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:18.340
It does one thing: make every actor's most selfish choice also be the most useful choice.

74
00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:25.140
Every dollar flows to maximum impact. Every researcher works on the highest-value problem.

75
00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:30.560
Every patient joins the trial that matters most. Nothing gets wasted.

76
00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:36.600
On your planet, you call this radical. On mine, we call it "obvious."

77
00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:43.460
It's been running longer than most of your civilizations have existed, though to be fair that's a low bar given how often

78
00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:51.620
yours collapse. It will do exactly three things: Receive funds (from the one percent Treaty, donations, etc.).

79
00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:59.560
Allocate research via patient subsidies (a market mechanism where sick people choose which trials to join and the money

80
00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:06.140
follows them) and infrastructure via Wishocracy (where everyone votes on which buildings to build).

81
00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:14.180
Verify results and pay proportional to impact (the part where you only get money if something actually worked, which is

82
00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:20.520
apparently a novel concept in your research sector). Everything operational will be outsourced.

83
00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:24.840
Trial infrastructure? Existing and new providers will compete for the work.

84
00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:30.740
Task decomposition? A.I. services. Talent matching? Existing marketplaces.

85
00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:37.080
The protocol itself will do almost nothing, on purpose. Why stay thin?

86
00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.520
Because thin protocols are hard to capture. There will be nothing to bribe.

87
00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:47.280
No operational role to corrupt. No C.E.O. to take on a yacht trip.

88
00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:55.920
Just rules that move money toward measured outcomes. Your species has a habit of corrupting every institution you build.

89
00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.560
You corrupt them the way water corrodes pipes: inevitably, given enough time and contact.

90
00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:06.700
The solution is to make the pipe so small and boring that nobody bothers.

91
00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:15.040
This one is boring enough that nobody bothers. Pay for Results (A Concept Your Research Sector Has Somehow Avoided).

92
00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:23.280
Every dollar will flow based on results, not promises. Patients will vote with their enrollment, and researchers will get

93
00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:31.400
paid for attracting them. Outcomes will determine continued funding, so campaigns that deliver will get more and failures

94
00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:39.780
will get defunded. Nobody gets paid for writing grant proposals, attending review committees, or publishing papers about

95
00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:45.180
why their research might work someday. This is how most other industries work.

96
00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.500
You pay contractors when they build the house, not when they promise to build it.

97
00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:55.680
You pay farmers when they grow the food, not when they apply for a farming license.

98
00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:00.120
Medicine, for reasons nobody can explain, went the other way.

99
00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.860
Your Data Commons: Publish Everything. Your current system hides failures.

100
00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:13.140
Companies bury negative results. Researchers don't publish what didn't work.

101
00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:21.360
Your scientists waste billions repeating mistakes someone else already made because they literally cannot find out those

102
00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:28.520
mistakes were already made. It's like your whole species has amnesia, but only for the embarrassing parts.

103
00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:34.180
You remember your triumphs in high definition and forget your failures completely.

104
00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:39.200
This is called "being human." It's also called "why you keep dying.".

105
00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:48.140
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will require one hundred percent open publication of all data, positive and negative,

106
00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:55.310
as a condition of funding. Every trial, every result, every dataset will be published.

107
00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:00.180
A.I. models will scan the global data commons, finding patterns humans miss.

108
00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.160
Failed experiments will become shared knowledge, not repeated waste.

109
00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:12.760
This is intelligence sharing in the Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, or S.H.A.E.F.

110
00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:18.340
analogy. Your Allies won partly because they shared Ultra intercepts across commands.

111
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:23.600
Your war on disease loses because everyone guards their failures like trade secrets.

112
00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:32.000
It's a group chat where everyone shares what didn't work. Except the group is your entire species and the topic is death

113
00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:38.720
prevention, which you'd think would motivate sharing, but doesn't, because your scientists are more afraid of looking wrong

114
00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:45.980
than of people staying dead. Governance: Sick People Choose, Everyone Else Votes.

115
00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:54.000
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will use two allocation mechanisms, because using one would be too simple and using

116
00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:00.340
three would be too complicated, and your species seems to operate best with exactly two of things.

117
00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:04.500
Two eyes, two hands, two political parties, two allocation mechanisms.

118
00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.320
Three confuses you. One bores you. Two is the human number.

119
00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:15.170
I don't know why. Maybe it's the eyes. Patient subsidies will handle research funding.

120
00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:20.000
Sick people will choose which trials to join. Money will follow their enrollment.

121
00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:27.040
This means research funding will be allocated by the people who will die if it doesn't work, which is a better selection

122
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:30.680
mechanism than a committee of people who will not die either way.

123
00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:39.320
Subsidies will be weighted by disease burden (disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s per patient), so severe conditions

124
00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:47.450
pay more per enrollee and mild ones pay less, which means every dollar buys the most healthy life-years possible.

125
00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:51.080
No committee will sit around deciding "cancer vs. Alzheimer's."

126
00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:57.440
The patients will decide, by showing up, and the price signal will make sure the money goes where the suffering is.

127
00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:06.140
Wishocracy will handle infrastructure and public goods. It will aggregate preferences through pairwise comparisons ("electronic

128
00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:14.740
health record, or E.H.R. integration or security audits?"), which is a fancy way of saying it asks everyone which of two

129
00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:19.240
things matters more, over and over, until a ranking emerges.

130
00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:25.680
Your species already does this when choosing restaurants. You just hadn't applied it to anything important.

131
00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:34.200
Making Selfishness Cure Disease. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health won't rely on altruism.

132
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:42.360
Altruism is lovely but flaky. It shows up when it feels like it, cancels last minute, and is always "busy that weekend."

133
00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:49.560
Instead, it will pay everyone to do the most useful thing, because greed is more dependable than kindness.

134
00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:55.660
Your entire economic system proves this daily. Nobody ever forgot to be greedy.

135
00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:59.900
Greed doesn't need a reminder app. It doesn't have a snooze button.

136
00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:05.920
It's the only human trait that works consistently, which is why it's the only one worth building on.

137
00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:14.480
Your researchers will get per-patient subsidies weighted by disease burden, so treating severe conditions will pay more

138
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:22.140
than treating mild ones. Your patients will get their trial costs covered, so joining experimental treatments costs nothing

139
00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:29.100
instead of everything. Your funders will get quadratic matching and outcome tracking, so high-R.O.I.

140
00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:37.040
donations will be amplified. Your data providers will get fees tied to data utility, so more useful data will mean more

141
00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:44.040
revenue. Every single actor's most selfish choice will also be the most useful choice.

142
00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:51.580
Selfishness, properly directed, will cure cancer. The civilizations I've worked with that figured this out are still around.

143
00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:58.600
The ones that decided selfishness was the problem and tried to build systems that required everyone to be nice are not.

144
00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:06.660
I don't say this to be mean. I say it because the archaeological record is very clear, and the archaeological record does

145
00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:15.720
not have feelings. How Your Researchers Get Paid. Your traditional system works like this: write a grant proposal, hope

146
00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:22.020
a committee likes it, get paid to try, maybe produce results, maybe not, get paid either way.

147
00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:28.480
It's one of the few professions on your planet where not doing the thing you're paid for has no consequences.

148
00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:36.960
(Actually, I've since learned this describes most of your government agencies, except those tend to get paid more for actively

149
00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.520
doing the opposite of their stated purpose, which is at least ambitious.).

150
00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:50.040
The new system: per-patient subsidies weighted by disease burden, with one equation that replaces your entire grant committee

151
00:16:50.040 --> 00:17:00.020
apparatus: Where is the subsidy per patient enrolled in a trial for condition is the disability-adjusted life years, or

152
00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:07.900
D.A.L.Y.s per patient for that condition (from your W.H.O.'s Global Burden of Disease data, which already exists and nobody

153
00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:15.720
was using for this), and is the value per D.A.L.Y. (a single constant, tuned to exhaust the annual budget).

154
00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:23.460
That's it. That's the whole allocation mechanism. The more patients who join your trial and the more severe the condition,

155
00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:27.339
the more funding you'll get. Patients vote with their enrollment.

156
00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:32.700
The price signal ensures every dollar buys the most healthy life-years possible.

157
00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:41.060
Cost is handled automatically: if the subsidy exceeds trial cost per patient, the trial is profitable and researchers will

158
00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:48.760
run it. If trial cost exceeds the subsidy, nobody runs it, which is correct, because it means the health value doesn't justify

159
00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:57.260
the expense. Researchers who find cheaper ways to run trials pocket the difference, which incentivizes efficiency instead

160
00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:00.880
of the current system, which incentivizes asking for more money.

161
00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:08.700
The only thing set centrally is which is just division: budget divided by global disease burden.

162
00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:16.620
Everything else is decentralized. Your entire species has been running grant committees for decades when you could have

163
00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:21.720
been running one equation. On Wishonia, this discovery took us about forty minutes.

164
00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:26.440
Your species has been at it for seventy years, which is not forty minutes.

165
00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:35.140
I've checked. Results-based continuation: deliver results and get more funding, or don't deliver and get defunded, which

166
00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:40.360
is how every other job on your planet works except, somehow, research.

167
00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:48.380
Pay your scientists like you pay your plumbers: for fixing the problem, not for explaining why the problem is hard.

168
00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:55.940
How It Works For Your Patients. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health won't replace your healthcare system.

169
00:18:56.840 --> 00:19:00.340
It will add an experimental treatment insurance layer on top.

170
00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:06.660
Your species already understands insurance. I've been trying to understand insurance for forty years.

171
00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:14.220
As far as I can tell, it's a system where you pay money every month so that when something bad happens, a different person

172
00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:21.380
can explain to you, using very long words, why the bad thing that happened isn't the kind of bad thing they meant.

173
00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:27.670
It's like a promise, but with lawyers, which on your planet means it's the opposite of a promise.

174
00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:32.420
This is similar, except it works. Sarah has Type Two Diabetes.

175
00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:39.560
Metformin isn't working. Under the old system, she has no other options covered and pays hundreds per month for a branded

176
00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:46.000
drug that's performing about as well as the drug that isn't working, which is a choice between two kinds of not working,

177
00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:53.880
which her species calls "options." With your Decentralized Institutes of Health, her doctor checks the trial network during

178
00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:58.940
a regular visit. Sees relevant trials. Recommends one based on Sarah's profile.

179
00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:03.280
Sarah enrolls with one click. The trial coverage pool covers all costs.

180
00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.740
Sarah pays a small copay. Gets an experimental drug that might work better.

181
00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:11.560
Reports blood sugar via app. Her data helps the next patient.

182
00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:18.680
Total time added to doctor visit: three minutes. Total time spent not dying: potentially the rest of her life.

183
00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:25.480
The ratio between these numbers seems favorable. Your doctors will cooperate because they get more treatment options for

184
00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:31.180
desperate patients, no liability (covered by trial insurance), and minimal workflow changes.

185
00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:38.400
Your patients will use it because they get affordable access to experimental treatments, doctor-recommended, with insurance-like

186
00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:46.100
coverage and no financial risk. It's like being a guinea pig, except the guinea pig gets paid, gets medicine, and consented

187
00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:50.360
to the arrangement, which distinguishes it from all previous guinea pig situations.

188
00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:56.880
How the Money Flows. The Architecture. The one percent Treaty Fund will hold the treasury.

189
00:20:57.580 --> 00:21:03.760
Money will come in from the one percent Treaty. It will allocate between infrastructure and public goods via Wishocracy.

190
00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:12.100
It will fund campaigns, not bureaucracies. It will have no C.E.O., no board, and no one to corrupt, (which will make it

191
00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:15.800
the first financial institution in your history with that property).

192
00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:22.720
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will be the thin coordination protocol that receives funding from the one percent

193
00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:28.600
Treaty Fund, allocates research via patient subsidies, verifies results, and pays for outcomes.

194
00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:33.060
It will be a set of rules, not a building. You won't visit it.

195
00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:36.580
You won't lobby it. You won't take it to lunch. This is the point.

196
00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:42.600
Trial infrastructure providers will be funded campaigns that compete to provide trial infrastructure.

197
00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:49.000
They won't be part of your Decentralized Institutes of Health; they'll be service providers with no budget authority, the

198
00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:54.620
same way a restaurant kitchen is not part of the building's plumbing even though they both involve pipes.

199
00:21:54.760 --> 00:22:02.700
The Fund Flow. Your one percent Treaty redirects twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year from global military budgets

200
00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:09.000
into the one percent Treaty Fund. Not all of it reaches research, because some of it goes to keeping the machine running,

201
00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:15.740
(which is how all machines work including your current ones, except this one admits it): Incentive Alignment Bonds receive

202
00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:23.800
ten percent plus ten percent, totaling two point seven two billion dollars plus two point seven two billion dollars, to

203
00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:32.300
align investors and politicians with outcomes. The research treasury receives eighty percent, totaling twenty-one point

204
00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:40.260
eight billion dollars, allocated via Wishocracy (approximately ninety-seven percent to disability-adjusted life years, or

205
00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:46.050
D.A.L.Y.-weighted patient subsidies, and approximately three percent to infrastructure).

206
00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:53.140
What Gets Funded: Market Failures Only. Most research allocation will happen automatically.

207
00:22:53.680 --> 00:23:00.180
Patients will choose trials, funding will flow there, and the market will handle it the way markets handle most things:

208
00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:08.800
imperfectly but vigorously. The one percent Treaty Fund will step in only for the things markets genuinely can't do.

209
00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:19.040
Infrastructure includes development and operations, competing alternative implementations, data commons storage and processing,

210
00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:24.320
security audits and fraud detection. These are the pipes and plumbing.

211
00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:30.300
Nobody builds pipes for fun. Markets don't produce pipes unless someone pays for them.

212
00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:39.540
So the fund pays for them. True public goods include patient trial participation subsidies, negative results publishing,

213
00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:47.740
and replication studies. These are the things nobody wants to pay for because the benefit is diffuse and the payer gets

214
00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:54.760
no credit. Your species has known about this problem since Olson described it, which was decades ago, and has done nothing

215
00:23:54.760 --> 00:24:00.640
about it, which is also decades ago. This is minimal by design.

216
00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:08.080
Your ecosystem will handle most research funding automatically: companies will register treatments, patients will join trials,

217
00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:16.660
revenue will flow, research will happen. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will only direct the one percent Treaty

218
00:24:16.660 --> 00:24:23.920
Fund to cover what the market genuinely can't, which is less than you think, because markets are surprisingly competent

219
00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:27.560
when you let sick people choose where to spend money on not being sick.

220
00:24:28.780 --> 00:24:33.370
What Your Decentralized Institutes of Health Outsources (Everything).

221
00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:38.010
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will be intentionally minimal.

222
00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:45.240
It will outsource everything operational because the best way to avoid corruption is to have nothing worth corrupting.

223
00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:50.990
If a provider stops performing, you'll fund a better one. This is how your species runs restaurants.

224
00:24:51.780 --> 00:25:00.140
Time to try it with medicine. Trial infrastructure will go to competing providers, because separate concerns stay uncorrupted.

225
00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:08.060
Crowdfunding will go to existing platforms, because they already exist and work (your species has already built functional

226
00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:11.940
crowdfunding; no need to reinvent it just because the cause is better).

227
00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:20.780
Talent matching will go to existing marketplaces. Data storage will go to competing providers, because market competition

228
00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:28.020
works better than monopoly, which you'd think would be obvious by now given that your entire economic philosophy is based

229
00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:31.580
on this observation and yet your government keeps forgetting it.

230
00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:42.340
Every outsourced function follows the same lifecycle: propose, vote via Wishocracy, receive funding, deliver, get measured,

231
00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:47.350
and earn continued funding based on results. This is called "accountability."

232
00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:55.380
Your species invented the word. I looked it up and it has existed in your language for over four hundred years.

233
00:25:55.980 --> 00:26:03.740
Time to try the concept. Anti-Capture Design. Your current system is trivially captured.

234
00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:09.140
Concentrate billions of dollars in a few committees, and lobbyists will find them.

235
00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:17.480
This is as predictable as gravity, and your species keeps being surprised by it, which is surprising, because the surprising

236
00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:24.260
thing is that you're surprised, which shouldn't be surprising because you've been surprised by it every time, which means

237
00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:29.700
being surprised is actually the normal thing, which means it's not surprising, which means...

238
00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:39.940
I've lost track. The point is lobbyists find money. They find it the way a dog finds a vacuum cleaner, except the dog runs

239
00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:48.580
away and the lobbyist runs toward it. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will make capture economically irrational

240
00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:54.920
by removing everything worth capturing. There will be no C.E.O., so there will be nothing to bribe.

241
00:26:55.360 --> 00:27:03.280
Governance rules will be encoded in transparent, auditable systems, and you can't bribe an algorithm (several of your corporations

242
00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:10.800
have tried; the algorithm didn't notice). Every dollar will be tracked on a public ledger, so corruption will be visible

243
00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:18.660
to everyone with an internet connection. Anyone will be able to build a competing alternative, so capturing one will just

244
00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:26.880
trigger the creation of another. Millions will vote via Wishocracy, so lobbying won't scale (you can bribe a committee of

245
00:27:26.930 --> 00:27:34.560
twelve; you cannot bribe a committee of twelve million, or rather you can, but it's called "taxation" and requires winning

246
00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:42.040
an election first). And funding will be determined by outcomes, so gaming the system will be harder than just performing

247
00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:48.340
well, which will be the first time in your species' history that a funding system has had that property.

248
00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:53.800
If any campaign provider gets captured, you'll fund a competing one instead.

249
00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:59.740
This is how your species handles bad restaurants, bad taxi companies, and bad barbers.

250
00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:07.900
It has never occurred to you to handle bad research institutions the same way, because you've classified them as "important,"

251
00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:14.700
and on your planet, "important" things are exempt from competition, which explains why they're all terrible.

252
00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:23.700
Security: Defense in Depth. A twenty-seven point two billion dollar treasury is a massive target.

253
00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:28.760
Every thief, hacker, and corrupt bureaucrat on your planet will try to steal from it.

254
00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:34.680
This is not speculation. This is a certainty, like sunrise or a politician lying.

255
00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:43.060
The defense will be four layers deep, because your species has repeatedly demonstrated that one layer is insufficient, two

256
00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:48.540
layers is insufficient, and three layers is insufficient, so four it is.

257
00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:53.380
Distributed control: No single person or committee will hold the keys.

258
00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:59.680
Community governance with time-delayed execution so fraud can be caught before funds move.

259
00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:09.160
This means stealing will require corrupting multiple independent parties simultaneously, which is expensive, and then waiting

260
00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:14.260
for the time delay, which is boring. Your species' thieves are impatient.

261
00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:24.720
This helps. Automated fraud detection: Real-time anomaly detection, duplication monitoring, and whistleblower bounties.

262
00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:28.890
Machines will watch the money so humans don't have to be trusted with it.

263
00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:37.120
This is not an insult to humans. This is a design choice based on four thousand, two hundred and ninety-seven years of watching

264
00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:42.900
humans near money. I've noticed that sick people would prefer not to be sick.

265
00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:47.800
This seems like useful information that your system has somehow overlooked.

266
00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:54.220
Full transparency: Every dollar will be tracked on public ledgers.

267
00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:59.100
Regular independent audits. You can't steal what everyone can see.

268
00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:04.320
Your species' most successful thieves have always relied on secrecy.

269
00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:11.420
Remove the secrecy and you remove the thieves, or at least make them very obvious, which is nearly as good.

270
00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:18.920
Recovery mechanisms: Clawbacks for data falsification. Emergency pause capabilities.

271
00:30:19.540 --> 00:30:24.520
If something goes wrong, the system will stop and fix itself before continuing.

272
00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:30.320
This is how your elevators work. When an elevator detects a problem, it stops.

273
00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:36.710
It does not continue delivering people to the wrong floor and then write a report about it afterward.

274
00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:43.940
Your financial systems should work like your elevators, but they don't, because your financial systems were designed by

275
00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:48.980
people who were paid to design them a certain way, and that way was not "well.".

276
00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:22.020
What You've Just Read. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health will not be a research institution, a trial platform, or

277
00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:22.700
a funding agency. It will be the thin layer that coordinates all of them.

278
00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:25.960
The one percent Treaty Fund will receive money from the treaty.

279
00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:26.332
Patient Patient subsidies will allocate research funding through a market mechanism where sick people choose trials.

280
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
Wishocracy will allocate infrastructure and public goods through democratic voting.

281
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
Trial infrastructure will provide clinical trial services through competing providers.

282
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
Incentive Alignment Bonds will align investors and politicians with outcomes through legal bribery.

283
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
The whole thing will receive funds, allocate research via patient subsidies, govern infrastructure via Wishocracy, verify

284
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
results, and pay proportional to outcomes. The highest-R.O.I.

285
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
action will become the selfish choice for every actor. Greed will cure cancer.

286
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
Selfishness will end disease. Your worst impulse will become your best medicine.

287
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
On Wishonia, we built this four thousand, two hundred and ninety-seven years ago.

288
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
We've been disease-free for four thousand, two hundred and ninety-six.

289
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
That first year was, admittedly, a bit rough. But you have the benefit of our mistakes, which we're sharing because sharing

290
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
mistakes freely is the first rule of the protocol, and also because we have four thousand, two hundred and ninety-six years

291
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
of evidence that it works, which is more evidence than your species has for anything except gravity and the observation

292
00:31:26.332 --> 00:31:26.332
that meetings could have been emails. That's the theory. The rest of this manual explains how you actually build it.
